// @run
// @log aa
// @log 1
// @log bb
// @log 1
// A STRING-keyed map whose value is a map with a REF value — `{[string]: {[string]:
// string}}`. This is a pre-existing defect fixed on the way to the i32-keyed ref-value
// work, and it is the string half of the same one line.
//
// `m[k] = Map()` never seeded the shape the inner `Map()` builds. That was silently
// right for exactly one case — an inner map whose value is MONO, whose struct IS the
// `-1` shape an unseeded constructor already builds — and silently WRONG for every
// other: the store `struct.new`'d the shared mono map struct into a vals slot typed to
// the inner map's OWN per-value struct. `vl check` rc 0, `vl build` writes a module
// that fails to instantiate (`type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found (ref
// $type)`). Every OTHER boundary that can construct a map — a `let`, a return, a
// global init, a struct field, a variant field — had already been taught its seed
// one at a time; the map-VALUE boundary was the one nobody had reached.
//
// The fix is `emitMapSetValExpr` seeding `svCtx.mapSlot = mvInnerMapShape(mslot)`, and
// it goes into the CONTEXT rather than the `pendingMapSlot` global: `emitExprExpect`
// writes the whole snapshot over the pending seeds, so a global written after
// `expCtxHere()` is overwritten before the value is emitted. (Diagnosed by writing it
// the wrong way first — the seed simply did not arrive.)
//
// The i32-keyed twin of this file is the last cell of `i32-keyed-ref-values.vl`.
function go() {
  const outer: {[string]: {[string]: string}} = Map()
  outer["a"] = Map()
  const inner = outer["a"] ?? Map()
  inner["x"] = "aa"
  print(inner["x"] ?? "z") // aa
  print(outer.length)      // 1

  // The same store through `.set`, and a read back through the outer map rather than
  // the bound inner — so the vals element's heap type is exercised on both sides of
  // the round trip.
  outer.set("b", Map())
  const inner2 = outer["b"] ?? Map()
  inner2["y"] = "bb"
  const back = outer["b"] ?? Map()
  print(back["y"] ?? "z") // bb
  print(back.length)      // 1
}
go()
